instructions: >
  Hand-authored producer (id dnsmasq): docs/architecture/<product>.yaml is the
  full logical architecture of one in-house app, and the source of truth; edit
  it directly. dnsmasq-config-generator.yaml models config-generator/,
  dnsmasq-management-api.yaml models management-api/. Both are validated in
  one call (cexec aac-tools arch-validate docs/architecture/*.yaml), each on its
  own: arch-validate resolves no reference. The management API's model
  consumes the config generator's svc: by id, so a change to that id is carried
  into the management API's model by hand; only the federation's validation
  report shows a dangling one.

  What this producer owns: each app's app:<name> SoftwareProduct identity
  (sourceRepository: git:pvginkel/Dnsmasq), the ApplicationService and
  ApplicationInterface it exposes, and the capabilities and services it
  consumes. DnsmasqDeploy (producer dnsmasq-deploy) owns the running instances
  that Specialize these products and the as-deployed wiring, so environment
  and cluster stay unset here. dnsmasq/ is a repackaged upstream image and is
  not modelled; its product is DnsmasqDeploy's ss:dnsmasq. tests/ and
  validation/ are test tooling and are not modelled either.

  Conventions. One ApplicationService per app, realized by the app (app
  -Realization-> svc), and one ApplicationInterface per distinct consumer of
  it (if -Assignment-> svc). Consumption edges are Associations from the
  consumer: a known in-house provider (management API -> config generator)
  targets that provider's svc:<id> directly, boundBy optional; a dependency
  located by an in-cluster service account (the Kubernetes API) targets its
  cap:<x> with no boundBy. When an app changes what it exposes or consumes,
  its model changes with it.
